course: world
lesson: 62
title: Volcanoes and earthquakes up close
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Under the Earth's thin rocky crust lies the mantle, hot rock that is solid but flows very slowly; the plates of the crust ride on it. Most volcanoes and earthquakes happen along the edges of these plates, especially around the Pacific Ocean, a belt called the Ring of Fire.
explain: A volcano erupts when magma, which is lighter than the solid rock around it and full of dissolved gas, rises into a chamber under the mountain; as it nears the surface the gas comes out of it in bubbles, like the gas in a shaken bottle of fizzy drink when the cap comes off.
explain: Runny lava lets gas escape gently, so it flows out in rivers, as in Hawaii; thick, sticky lava traps the gas until it blasts out in an explosion of ash and rock, as at Mount Vesuvius, which buried the Roman town of Pompeii in the year 79.
explain: In an earthquake, rocks on both sides of a crack called a fault are pushed for years, bend a little, and then suddenly slip; the energy spreads out as waves through the ground. The place underground where the slip starts is the focus, and the point on the surface right above it is the epicentre.
explain: An earthquake under the sea can lift the sea floor and push up a huge wave called a tsunami, which crosses the ocean as fast as a jet plane; if the sea suddenly pulls far back from the beach, people must run to high ground at once.
fact: The Earth has a thin rocky crust, a thick hot mantle and a very hot core in the middle.
fact: Most volcanoes and earthquakes are found where the Earth's plates meet.
fact: The Ring of Fire is a belt of many volcanoes and earthquakes around the Pacific Ocean; Japan, Indonesia and Chile lie on it.
fact: Magma rises because it is lighter than the solid rock around it.
fact: Gas trapped in sticky magma makes explosive eruptions; runny lava flows out more gently.
fact: An active volcano has erupted in recent times and may erupt again; an extinct volcano is not expected to erupt ever again.
fact: A fault is a crack in the Earth's crust where rocks can slip.
fact: The epicentre is the point on the surface directly above where an earthquake starts.
fact: Scientists measure earthquakes with instruments called seismometers.
fact: A tsunami is a series of huge waves, usually caused by an earthquake under the sea.
fact: Volcanic ash breaks down into very fertile soil, so many farms lie near volcanoes.
fact: Nobody can yet predict the exact day of an earthquake, but strong buildings save many lives.
fact: Buildings made to bend and sway a little stand up to earthquakes better than stiff brick ones.
fact: Vesuvius had been quiet for so long that the people of Pompeii did not know it was a dangerous volcano.
fact: Out in the deep ocean a tsunami is low, but in shallow water near the coast it slows down and piles up into a huge wave.
exercise: Where do most volcanoes and earthquakes happen? | Along the edges of the Earth's plates | In the middle of the largest continents | Only near the North and South Poles | Wherever the weather is hottest
exercise: What is the Ring of Fire? | A belt of volcanoes and earthquakes around the Pacific Ocean | A ring of hot deserts around the equator | A circle of red-hot lava flowing all the way around the Earth's core | The burning ring around the Sun
exercise: Why does magma rise towards the surface? | It is lighter than the solid rock around it | The Sun's heat pulls it upward through cracks in the ground | The wind sucks it out of the ground like a straw | The oceans press it up from below with their weight
exercise: Why do some volcanoes explode while others pour out lava gently? | Sticky magma traps gas until it bursts out | Explosive volcanoes are always the tallest ones | Gentle volcanoes have no magma inside them | Explosions happen only when it rains
exercise: What is the epicentre of an earthquake? | The point on the surface right above where it starts | The deepest point the shaking reaches | The city where the most damage is done, even if it is far away | The place farthest from where it starts
exercise: What is a fault? | A crack in the crust where rocks can slip | A hole at the top of a volcano where lava pours out | A hollow under the ground full of hot lava | A mistake made when measuring an earthquake
exercise: What causes an earthquake? | Rocks along a fault suddenly slip after being pushed for a long time | Heavy rain soaks the ground and makes it shake | Strong winds rock the mountains back and forth | The Moon pulls the ground up once a year
exercise: What instrument measures earthquakes? | A seismometer | A thermometer | A barometer | A telescope
exercise: What usually causes a tsunami? | An earthquake under the sea | A strong wind over the ocean | A very high tide at full moon | A big ship sailing too fast
exercise: What should you do if the sea suddenly pulls far back from a beach? | Run to high ground at once | Go out to collect the shells | Wait on the beach to watch | Swim out to the deep water
exercise: Why do many people farm near volcanoes? | Volcanic ash makes very fertile soil | Volcanoes keep the land free of insects | It never rains near a volcano | Lava waters the fields every spring
exercise: What is an extinct volcano? | One that is not expected to erupt ever again | One that is erupting right now with lava and ash | One that erupts once every single year | One that has no mountain around it at all
exercise: What are the layers of the Earth from the outside in? | Crust, mantle, core | Core, mantle, crust | Mantle, crust, core | Crust, core, mantle
exercise: Why does a shaken bottle of fizzy drink help explain an eruption? | Gas dissolved in a liquid bursts out when the pressure drops | Fizzy drinks are as hot as lava when they come out of the bottle | The bottle is shaped like a volcano | Sugar in the drink is like ash
test: Japan, Indonesia and Chile all have many earthquakes. What do they share? | They lie on the Ring of Fire where plates meet | They are all in the hottest part of the world, near the equator | They all lie far from any ocean | They all have very large deserts
test: Lava in Hawaii flows like a slow red river, but Mount St Helens blew its top off. Why the difference? | Hawaii's lava is runny and lets gas escape; the other magma was sticky and trapped it | Hawaii's volcano is much smaller and weaker | The other volcano had water instead of lava | Hawaii's lava is cold and the other was hot
test: Which is true about predicting earthquakes? | Scientists know where they are likely, but not the exact day | Scientists can tell the exact hour weeks ahead of every quake | Earthquakes come only at night, so they are very easy to predict | Animals always warn us a day before, so we can simply watch them
test: Two towns are hit by the same strong earthquake. In one, buildings are made to bend and sway; in the other, they are stiff brick. Which suffers less? | The town with buildings made to bend and sway | The town with stiff brick buildings | Both suffer exactly the same | Neither, because earthquakes do not harm buildings
test: Why did the people of Pompeii have so little warning? | The volcano had been quiet for so long that they did not know it was dangerous | The eruption happened under the sea | The volcano was hidden behind clouds | Volcanoes in Roman times erupted without any ash
test: A ship far out in the deep ocean hardly notices a tsunami passing under it. Why does the same wave become huge at the coast? | In shallow water the wave slows down and piles up higher | The wind grows stronger near the coast and pushes it higher | The water near the coast is saltier and heavier, so it rises | The wave turns round at the shore and doubles itself in size
test: If the mantle stopped flowing, what would happen to the plates? | They would stop moving, so mountains would stop rising | They would sink into the hot core at once and melt away | They would move faster than before | They would float up into space
test: What would you find right on the epicentre of an earthquake? | The point of the surface directly above where the rocks slipped | The hole the lava comes out of | The deepest point of the fault, where the rocks first start to slip | The edge where the shaking stops
test: A mountain has no record of ever erupting and its crater is worn away and covered by forest. Which word fits it best? | Extinct | Active | Erupting | About to erupt
test: why do so many farms sit right next to volcanoes, isnt that dumb | Volcanic ash breaks down into very rich, fertile soil | Volcanoes keep the fields warm all through the cold winter | Lava scares away the insects and pests that eat the crops | Volcanoes bring extra rain to all the fields nearby
test: so what even is the epicentre of a quake | The point on the surface right above where the quake starts | The deepest point where the quake starts underground | The place where the quake does the most damage | The middle of the plate where the quake ends
test: Mia says scientists can tell the exact day an earthquake will hit. true? | No, nobody can yet predict the exact day, so strong buildings matter | Yes, seismometers can warn of every single quake at least a week ahead | Yes, but only for quakes that happen under the sea | No, scientists cannot measure earthquakes at all
test: a video said magma comes up cos the volcano sucks it out. right? | No, magma rises because it is lighter than the solid rock around it | Yes, the volcano pulls magma up like a straw | Yes, the wind over the top draws the magma up | No, magma rises because the Sun heats the ground
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the location of the majority of volcanoes and earthquakes? | They occur chiefly where the Earth's plates meet | They occur chiefly in the centres of the largest plates | They occur chiefly near the North and South Poles | They occur with equal frequency in all places on Earth
exam: Identify the principal reason for which certain eruptions are of an explosive character. | Gas is trapped within sticky magma | The lava is exceptionally runny | The volcano is wholly extinct | The magma is heavier than the surrounding rock
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the designation epicentre. | The point upon the surface directly above the origin of an earthquake | The point deep underground at which an earthquake begins | The crater at the summit of a volcano | The instrument by which earthquakes are measured
exam: Ascertain the cause by which a tsunami is most usually occasioned. | An earthquake beneath the sea | A strong wind over the ocean | The pull of the Moon upon the tides | Heavy rainfall upon the coast
